and we write {p(x)=$(x^3+x+1/p(x)+1)(x^3+x+1/p(x)-1)+(1/(x^3+x+1/p(x)-1))/x^2 \\
=x^3+(1/(\frac{x^3}{x^3+x+1/p(x)-1}+1)+1=\alpha}
and for this post, this part mean 
that we solve the question like \'][$(\alpha)=0,$ we obtain $1+2/k^2+\sum _{k=1}^N k^3=0$ where $\sum_{i=1}^Ni^3=p^2 +1/12,N ∈ Z^+,$ while $p$ is a sum of divisors of $\prod _{i=1}^Nk $, and we can prove this by induction}



(answer) In terms of (1) and using the above observation with $\sum _{k=1}^N k =N(N+1)/2,$ we get  $\sum _{k=1}^Nk ^3=N ^2(N+1)^2/4+(1+2k^2+k^4).$ Then our equation becomes$$N ^2(N+1)^2/4+(1+2k^2+k^4)=p^2 +1/12$$$$N ^2/N^2(N+1)^2=\alpha=\sum_{N=0}^\infty N(N+1)/2=k^2.$$Thus we see$$N(N+1)/2=k^3,\: k=1\Rightarrow N=1.$$So $p=1.$ We now see that $p(x)=$ $(x^2+x+1)^2-x^2+1=x^4+3x^2+2,$ and therefore, by Vieta,$$-(coefficient of x^2)/coefficient of x^4=-3/1=3.$$

